    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Amid intensifying tensions between the United States and China, President Biden has reaffirmed the U.S.’s commitment to defending Taiwan against a Chinese invasion. However, it remains uncertain whether widespread American public support exists for such military intervention, or if public opinion aligns with those of political elites. This thesis examines American public and political elite opinions regarding potential American intervention in Taiwan in the event of a Chinese invasion.  \r\n\r\nTo gauge public sentiment, I conducted a unique survey experiment presenting a representative sample of the American public with one of four hypothetical scenarios depicting a Chinese invasion of Taiwan. I varied the baseline scenario to assess the impact of three key factors: elite consensus favoring intervention, a Taiwanese declaration of independence, and allied support for defending Taiwan. To capture elite opinion, I interviewed current and former senior policymakers at the forefront of American national security decision-making.\r\n\r\nThe findings reveal that a majority of both the public and political elites support intervention if China invades Taiwan without provocation, with a shared preference for non-kinetic approaches. However, if Taiwan declares independence, approximately half of the elite respondents who initially favored intervention shift to opposition, whereas public opinion remains largely unchanged. Additionally, while political elites unanimously view the threat of using nuclear weapons as a red line, a non-insignificant portion of the public supports nuclear threats to defend Taiwan. These findings suggest that political elites may place a higher value on adherence to international norms than the general public does, highlighting a potential disconnect that warrants further exploration, especially as American policymakers balance security commitments with cautious foreign policy.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
